@node DDIR
@chapter DDIR

DDIR, the Differential Dataflow Intermediate Representation, is a small
language for building differential dataflows at runtime.  Where the
collection API (@pxref{Differential Dataflow}) builds a dataflow in C at
compile time, DDIR builds one from a text program, so a host can accept
dataflow definitions as data.

A program names positional input collections, defines derived
collections by expressions (map and filter, equijoin, reduce, concat,
and @code{iterate} for recursion), and declares named outputs.  Its time
is @code{Product<u64, PointStamp<u64>>}, so iteration pushes a loop
coordinate without changing the timestamp type.

@section Two surface syntaxes

The same program can be written two ways, which parse to the same IR.
The @dfn{applicative} syntax (@file{.ddir}) nests function-like forms;
the @dfn{pipe} syntax (@file{.ddp}) threads a value through a pipeline
of operators.  Reachability, in both:

@example
-- applicative (.ddir)
let edges = MAP(INPUT 0, ($0[0] ; $0[1]));
let roots = MAP(INPUT 1, ($0[0] ;));
reach: @{
    let proposals = JOIN(reach, edges, ($2));
    var reach = REDUCE(CONCAT(roots, proposals), DISTINCT);
@}
export "result" = INSPECT(ARRANGE(MAP(reach::reach, (;))), total);
@end example

@example
-- pipe (.ddp)
let edges = input 0 | key($0[0] ; $0[1]);
let roots = input 1 | key($0[0] ;);
reach: @{
    let proposals = reach | join(edges, ($2 ;));
    var reach = roots + proposals | distinct;
@}
export "result" = reach::reach | key(;) | arrange | inspect(total);
@end example

@section The pipeline

@table @code
@item dtfl_ddir_parse
turns program text in a chosen syntax into a lowered, optimized
scope-tree program (the IR).
@item dtfl_ddir_build
lowers that IR into a live differential dataflow inside a scope, binding
the program's positional inputs to collections and returning its named
exports; the scope's time class must be @code{dtfl_ddir_time_class}.
@item dtfl_ddir_evaluate
is the data-in, data-out shortcut: it feeds explicit input rows, runs
the program to quiescence on one in-process worker, and returns each
export's consolidated final contents.
@end table

A DDIR value is an algebraic data type over a single scalar: an integer,
a tuple, a tagged variant, or a list.  A collection element is a
@code{(key, value)} pair of such values.  The @code{dtfl_ddir_value_*}
functions construct and inspect values, and @code{dtfl_ddir_value_parse}
parses one from a closed scalar term.

@section The explanation rewrite

DDIR can rewrite a program so that, for a query against its first
export, it computes the input rows that regenerate the queried output
row: a per-source demand-set explanation.  @code{dtfl_ddir_explain}
produces that rewritten program; @code{dtfl_ddir_clone_identity} is the
identity transform it is built on.

@section The command-line tool

@command{dtfl-ddir} builds and runs DDIR programs.  @command{dtfl-ddir
run @var{program} [@var{rows-file}]} evaluates a program on explicit
rows and prints each export's sorted contents; @command{dtfl-ddir
server} runs a live command session (install, feed, tick, peek);
@command{dtfl-ddir explain} runs the explanation rewrite.  See
@code{dtfl-ddir(1)} for the row and session syntax.
